      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 American College of Allergy, Asthma & Immunology. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Total specific airway resistance (sRtot) has been introduced as an alternative technique to assess lung function with a particular application to younger children with asthma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To establish a diagnostic value of the body plethysmographic parameter (sRtot) in asthma diagnosis in young children."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "This was a prospective, noninterventional study. Children 4 to 18 year old with symptoms suggestive of asthma were included (n = 885). Subjects underwent body plethysmography and spirometry (when capable) with reversibility tests. Of 788 subjects who could perform spirometry in addition to body plethysmography, 578 were diagnosed with asthma. Subjects with asthma were treated for minimum of 6 months and then their asthma was confirmed or refuted."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "In 471 patients, asthma diagnosis was confirmed after 6 months of antiasthmatic treatment; 142 patients were 4 to 6 years old and 329 were 7 to 18 years old. Change in response to bronchodilator in children with asthma was significant for sRtot (P = .02) but not for forced expiration volume in 1 second (P = .21); sRtot was more sensitive and specific in identifying children with reversible obstruction than spirometry. There was a significant association between sRtot and asthma diagnosis in patients 4 to 6 years old (odds ratio 1.02, 95% confidence interval 1.01-1.03, P = .001); to differentiate subjects with asthma from those without asthma, the optimal cutoff point for sRtot was 174.5%. A sRtot value higher than 174.5% was associated with a positive prediction of an asthma diagnosis in patients 4 to 6 years old. A ratio of forced expiration volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity below 80% was not significantly associated with asthma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "These data support the recommendation of performing sRtot rather than spirometry in young children as a fairly sensitive marker of asthma."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "TRIAL REGISTRATION",
            "content": "www.ClinicalTrials.gov (NCT01805635)."
          }
        ]
      },
